  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1015 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nTuesday opens with widespread low stratus and maybe some fog that \neventually scatters out with daytime mixing allowing highs to reach \nthe 70s area wide. Despite muggy southerly winds both Tuesday and \nWednesday, drier and more stable air aloft will thwart rain chances \nthese days. These moist breezes may give rise to the dreaded sunrise-\nsurprise stratus Wednesday morning ahead of clearing skies and even \nmilder highs in the upper 70s and lower 80s. Aloft meanwhile, steady \nheight rises unfold in SW flow through Wednesday ahead of the next \nstorm system emanating from an active subtropical jet. Already moist \nlow levels with dewpoints holding in the 50s will coincide with \nrichening moisture aloft to result in rain chances from Thursday \nthrough Saturday morning, with the peak in these chances contingent \non the timing of a southern stream trough. Ensembles currently favor \nFriday as the wettest day complete with some heavy rain potential. \n\nNo cold fronts are in our future until early November at the \nsoonest, so enjoy the pleasant temperatures as we can certainly be \ntalking cold and snow already in late October. In fact, fifty years \nago this week our region saw its earliest fall snowstorm with 7.5\" \nmeasured at Lubbock on the 28th and 29th!",
